d move here. It immediately occupies the center, exploits Black’s missing d7 pawn (no pawn to contest d4 or defend d5), opens lines for both bishops and the queen, and gives White a strong pawn duo (d4 and f5). This move also prepares for quick development of the remaining minor pieces with tempo-gaining possibilities. It’s a standard and strong opening move that maximizes pressure and controls the game from the start.  \n\nAnswer: P: d2-d4"}
