es multiple major threats:  \n- It attacks the rook on a7 with no immediate defender.  \n- It prepares Qxe7+ (after Black moves rook) leading to quick mate or massive material gain.  \n- It limits Black's king movement and forces awkward defensive moves.  \nGiven Black’s king safety is poor and White’s pieces are coordinated for an attack, this aggressive queen move likely forces decisive material loss or checkmate.  \n\n**Answer**: Q: d1-d7"}
