elessly.  \n3. **K: e1-c1** – queenside castling, getting king safer and connecting rooks.  \n4. **N: c3-e4** – centralizing knight, eyeing d6 and f6 squares.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nGiven the position, the pawn on c5 is advanced; pushing **c6** disrupts Black’s pawn structure and can create weaknesses around b7 and d6. If Black captures en passant (b7xc6), our b-file opens against Black’s rook, and we get a passed pawn on c6 after recapture. If Black doesn’t capture, we have a strong wedge and can support it with b4, etc. It also restricts Black’s pieces. Compared to castling immediately, this maintains initiative and creates immediate problems for Black, which is consistent with White’s opening advantage. Other moves like Bb5 or Ne4 are fine but less forcing.\n\n**Answer:** P: c5-c6'}
